fxfile
======

Copyright (c) 2013 Leon Lee Author. All rights reserved.

Overview
--------
fxfile (formerly flyExplorer) is a free, open source file manager for Microsoft Windows.

Modern Build Environment
------------------------
The default build path targets Visual Studio 2026 and the v145 platform toolset.

Required software:

1. Visual Studio 2026
   - Install the "Desktop development with C++" workload.
   - Install MFC for the current MSVC v145 x64/x86 build tools.
   - Install a current Windows 10/11 SDK.
2. Python 3.9 or newer.
3. Git.

The old Python 2.7.4 and Visual Studio 2015/v140 toolchain are no longer required
for the default build path.

Development Policy and Fast Preflight
-------------------------------------
The repository vendors the validated `universal-agent-docs` policy bundle under
`.agent-policy/`. Project-specific instructions remain in `AGENTS.md` and
project facts in `.agent-policy/PROJECT.md`.

Before a C++ checkpoint, run:

    python build/validate_source_invariants.py

`build.bat` runs this preflight automatically before downloading tools,
deleting stale generated projects, or running GYP. The current preflight checks
direct header dependencies for cross-cutting ModernUI symbols, verifies the
DPI/manifest integration points, and carries deterministic regression guards for
known UI and product-integration failures. It prevents standard non-resizing dialogs from
drifting back to direct `CDialog` inheritance outside the specialized allowlist,
checks the ThemePalette/DPI wiring of custom-painted About/Tip/PictureViewer
surfaces, and protects toolbar/config-tree bitmap frame geometry plus the
application-icon frame contract. It also rejects the unsupported ToolbarWindow32
`TB_SETBKCOLOR`/`TB_SETTEXTCOLOR` identifiers and verifies that toolbar
dark-state colors remain routed through `NM_CUSTOMDRAW`/`NMTBCUSTOMDRAW`. The
same preflight also protects native-test wiring for rename transforms,
fnmatch/FilePath, ConfFile persistence, updater version/metadata/command
contracts, and the isolated installer smoke path. It is a static check and does
not replace a Visual Studio compile/link build.

Policy/readiness checks can be run with:

    python -m pip install --require-hashes --requirement .agent-policy/requirements.lock
    python .agent-policy/scripts/validate.py
    python .agent-policy/scripts/validate.py --project-root . --readiness development
    python .agent-policy/scripts/conformance.py

These checks remain available for local/manual use. GitHub Actions execution is
restricted indefinitely: every individual run requires an explicit instruction
from the user for that run. The previous 2026-09-30 budget-pause date does not
authorize automatic restart or manual dispatch. A policy/static PASS must not
be reported as a product compile, integration, or runtime PASS.

Windows Product Build CI
------------------------
Automatic push/pull-request triggers remain disabled without an expiration date.
Do not restore them merely because a date passes or a budget resets. Each
manual dispatch requires the user's explicit instruction for that individual
run as well as the `run_ci` override; prior approval, commits and pushes do
not count as permission.

The Actions workflows are intentionally slim:

- `.github/workflows/policy-validation.yml` now runs only
  `build/validate_source_invariants.py`. The fuller vendored-policy validation,
  readiness, and conformance commands remain available for local/manual use.
- `.github/workflows/windows-build.yml` defaults to one
  `Release-x64-Unicode | x64` job. It regenerates the solution, checks the
  v145/MFC contract, compiles and links the product, rejects compiler/linker/
  MSBuild warnings, verifies the core artifacts, runs the isolated native
  product functional regression (filesystem operations, rename,
  pattern/path, config persistence, updater contracts, hash and shell-command
  checks), and then runs the x64 launch smoke.
- The x86-compatibility workflow input is retained for future/manual use, but
  the current project CI scope does **not** permit enabling it unless the user
  explicitly expands the validation scope.
- Debug x64, x86 compatibility, package generation/validation, Actions artifact
  staging/upload, and the previous full policy corpus are not part of the
  active CI scope.

A successful default Windows Product Build is Release-x64 compile/link, warning,
artifact, and hosted-runner launch evidence only. GitHub's Windows runner already
contains Visual Studio and other system software, so it is not clean-Windows
Sandbox/VM evidence and does not replace interactive functional or visual
regression testing.

Local CI
--------
The active development/CI build scope is currently **Release-x64-Unicode | x64
only**. Use `local_ci.bat x64` (or the no-argument default, which is the same
profile):

    local_ci.bat
    local_ci.bat x64

The `x64` profile regenerates the VS 2026 x64 solution, runs the source
invariant/regression preflight, **rebuilds Release-x64-Unicode from source**,
runs the native functional regression, rejects compiler/linker/MSBuild warnings,
verifies core artifacts, and runs the x64 launch smoke.

Other profiles remain implemented as maintenance assets but are **not part of
the active CI scope**. Do not run them for normal development unless the user
explicitly expands the validation scope:

    local_ci.bat quick
    local_ci.bat test
    local_ci.bat install
    local_ci.bat policy
    local_ci.bat x86
    local_ci.bat release
    local_ci.bat full

In particular, Debug-x64, x86 Unicode/Multibyte, `release`, `full`, and the
GitHub Actions x86-compatibility job are inactive by current project direction.
The utility profiles are retained so they can be used for a specifically
requested static, installer, policy, compatibility, or release investigation
without reconstructing the tooling.

Local CI deliberately uses MSBuild `Rebuild` rather than incremental `Build`
so stale object files cannot hide CRT/configuration drift and compiled `.cpp`
files remain visible in the console using MSBuild minimal verbosity (similar to
ordinary Visual Studio build output), while the file logger keeps normal detail
for diagnostics.

The `policy`/`full` profiles create or reuse
`%TEMP%\\flyExplorer-policy-venv` and install the locked policy dependencies
there. This directory is an isolated Python virtual environment, not a log
directory. Local CI writes one consolidated log to `logs\\local-ci\\local-ci.log` in the
repository root; `logs/` is ignored by Git. Each run replaces the previous
local CI log, and temporary capture files are deleted when the run finishes.
See `LOCAL_CI_MANUAL.txt` for the short profile-by-profile usage guide.
`local_ci.bat` forces redirected Python output to UTF-8 so readiness and
conformance messages containing Unicode punctuation do not fail on Korean or
other non-UTF-8 Windows ANSI code pages.
MSBuild file-logger output is separately normalized from the Windows ANSI code
page to BOM-less UTF-8 before it is printed and appended to `local-ci.log`, so
localized Korean build messages do not corrupt the consolidated UTF-8 log.
The local conformance invocation also suppresses only Python `zipfile`'s expected
`Duplicate name` warning from the intentional duplicate-entry negative fixture;
the duplicate-entry validation case itself still runs and must pass.
Local CI does not publish artifacts or modify GitHub Actions state. On a developer
machine, runtime smoke refuses to overwrite existing flyExplorer HKCU startup or
profile state. When such state is present, `local_ci.bat` records that smoke as
SKIPPED/NOT TESTED and continues the remaining local checks. Installer execution
is also opt-in: without `FXFILE_INSTALLER_SMOKE=1`, or without an elevated token,
the installer contract is inspected but actual install/uninstall is recorded as
SKIPPED/NOT TESTED. GitHub-hosted launch smoke keeps the strict behavior and still
fails on unexpected pre-existing state.

Hosted runtime smoke can also be invoked manually after a build, for example:

    pwsh -File build\runtime_smoke.ps1 -SourceDir bin\x64 -Configuration Release-x64-Unicode

Manual Runtime Smoke Checklist
------------------------------
For an interactive Windows or Sandbox check, keep destructive file operations
out of scope and verify at least:

1. Startup: the main window appears and no crash/missing-DLL dialog is shown.
2. Folder tree: expand Desktop, select a drive, and navigate to a test folder.
3. File list: enter a child folder and navigate back to its parent.
4. AddressBar: confirm the current path, type a test path, press Enter, open the
   dropdown, and confirm basic autocomplete behavior.
5. Tabs: create, switch, and close a tab.
6. Window behavior: minimize, restore, resize, and maximize.
7. Helpers: note launcher/keyhook/updater behavior without installing updates or
   modifying shell integration.

Record Windows Sandbox/clean-VM, interactive functional, and visual results
separately from the hosted-runner launch smoke.

Windows 11 UI Modernization
---------------------------
The current UI modernization keeps the MFC/Win32 architecture while reusing the
shared `gui/ModernUI` DPI, system-typography, and system-palette foundation.
The status-bar layer now uses the system UI font, 96-DPI logical pane metrics
that are rescaled for the current monitor, flat ThemePalette-based
background/text/separators, and settings/theme/DPI refresh handling.

`ExplorerStatusBar` keeps its existing four-pane information contract and
drive-properties double-click behavior. Its drive-usage progress remains the
existing GDI `TextProgressCtrl`, but uses DPI-aware system typography,
palette-derived fill/track/text colors, bounded percentage rendering, and
DPI-scaled insets. Other StatusBar consumers share the generic typography,
palette, and DPI metric fixes without adopting Explorer-specific progress
styling.

The first dialog-specific modernization reference is `DriveDetailDlg`. It
applies the current-DPI system UI font to its labels and value fields, preserves
the existing bold percentage text in the shared `TextProgressCtrl`, refreshes
typography and custom-control appearance for settings/theme/DPI notifications,
and uses ThemePalette colors plus DPI-scaled metrics in `DrivePieCtrl`.
Drive-data queries, localized strings, icon behavior, and the dialog's existing
information structure remain unchanged.

The main top-level frame now opts into the documented Windows 11 DWM
`DWMWA_USE_IMMERSIVE_DARK_MODE` window-frame behavior through a dynamically
resolved ModernUI helper. The frame request is applied after creation and
refreshed for settings/theme notifications; High Contrast keeps the existing
light-frame fallback, and unsupported/down-level DWM calls fail without adding a
load-time dependency.

ModernUI now also provides common-control theme helpers that dynamically
resolve the documented `SetWindowTheme` API. `applyExplorerControlTheme`
applies the Windows Explorer visual-style association while High Contrast falls
back to system control rendering, and `resetControlTheme` preserves explicit
theme-disable behavior. `ListCtrlEx` and `TreeCtrlEx` now route their existing
`enableVistaEnhanced` enable/disable contract through these helpers instead of
calling `SetWindowTheme` directly.

MainFrame already reapplies its top-level frame theme and redraws child windows
for settings/theme changes. The legacy rebar MenuBar and ToolBarEx keep their
WinXP-only `SetWindowThemeXP(..., L"", L"")` behavior because an empty theme
association is not equivalent to the Explorer sub-app theme; MenuBar reopens its
theme handle on `WM_THEMECHANGED`, while ToolBarEx refreshes its modern metrics
for DPI/settings/theme notifications.

For the explorer file list/header styling, ModernUI now also reads the documented
Windows Runtime UISettings foreground/background colors to reflect the user's app
light/dark preference in ThemePalette. High Contrast stays on the system-color
path, and unavailable WinRT activation or color queries fall back to the existing
system-color palette. ExplorerCtrl's thumbnail/custom-draw colors and image-list
background now share this palette contract, while FlatHeaderCtrl keeps its existing
ThemePalette-based custom drawing.

The main explorer dark-styling units now include file list/header, folder tree,
AddressBar/edit/combo, auxiliary shell controls, and MenuBar/Rebar/Toolbar.
MenuBar keeps its existing toolbar custom-draw/menu-tracking ownership while dark
normal/hot/selected/disabled colors come from ThemePalette. CToolBarEx keeps the
native ToolbarWindow32/image-list/command structure and applies the same palette
to the main toolbar and chevron popup. The shared CSizableReBar host now supplies
palette-backed rebar background/text/non-client borders for both menu and toolbar
rebars. BCMenu's actively used Windows8/Standard owner-draw popup paths also use
ThemePalette in dark mode, while Light and High Contrast retain system/native
colors. The exact-Windows-XP empty-theme compatibility path remains unchanged.
Broader dialog modernization now has shared coverage for the major reusable
dialog families. CResizingDialog supplies current-DPI system typography,
ThemePalette-backed dark surfaces/edit-list colors, common-control theme
association, and settings/theme/DPI refresh to the resizable command-dialog
family. TitleDlg applies the same foundation to create/rename/shortcut/file-list
dialogs while keeping its custom descriptive header and drawing resource icons at
a DPI-scaled 32 logical pixels. The configuration UI now shares the same contract
through CfgDlg pages and CfgMainDlg, including palette-backed config-tree colors.

ModernUI's top-level frame helper now follows the actual ThemePalette light/dark
state instead of requesting a dark frame for every non-High-Contrast window.
ModernUI also exposes a DPI-aware resource-icon loader, and the command dialogs
that owned the legacy IDR_MAINFRAME title icon now load that icon after HWND
creation at the current monitor DPI. Standard non-resizing dialogs now share the
new `CModernDialog` base for system typography, ThemePalette surfaces,
common-control theme association, top-level frame theme, and
settings/theme/DPI refresh. Specialized owners such as `CResizingDialog`,
`TitleDlg`, `CfgDlg`/`CfgMainDlg`, and `DriveDetailDlg` keep their
dedicated contracts.

Custom-painted `AboutDlg`, `TipDlg`, `PictureViewer`, and toolbar customize
surfaces now use ThemePalette/DPI-aware paths instead of fixed light-only
backgrounds. The application `fxfile.ico` preserves its existing 16/32/48
frames and adds a 32-bit 256px frame for high-DPI shell use. `tip.ico` and
`shortcut.ico` now carry 32/40/48/64px frames, while `goup.ico` preserves its
legacy 16/32 variants and adds 40/48/64px 32-bit frames. Parent-folder rendering
uses the shared DPI-aware icon loader instead of fixed-size `LoadIcon` drawing.
Main toolbar still preserves the historical 51-frame command/index and bitmap
fallback contract, but a manifest-driven Microsoft Fluent registry now overlays
23 command frames at runtime: every command enabled by default plus optional
History and Settings. App-owned BCMenu reuses the same command-ID artwork, and
the settings navigation tree overlays 17 strong semantic matches while retaining
its 33-frame bitmap as High-Contrast/legacy fallback. Frame counts, cell geometry,
mask format, RC/GYP registration and generated-mask determinism remain protected
by automated resource-contract checks.

The shared dialog/icon modernization has source-level static coverage, and the
preflight now includes deterministic regression guards for direct-dialog drift,
custom-paint regressions, toolbar API/custom-draw wiring, and UI resource
contracts.

The 2026-09-23 runtime/UI stabilization pass is also integrated into `main`.
Control-theme association changes are idempotent, settings `WM_THEMECHANGED`
handling avoids reapplying child theme associations, shell registration is
per-user under HKCU, single-instance detection no longer requires all-access
mutex rights, protected-path configuration/accelerator migration copies into the
per-user destination, and the cross-integrity wake-up path allows only the
registered activation message when the documented API is available. AddressBar
nested borders, legacy coolbar sizing, owner-draw Dark colors/fonts, filtered
list default text, and app-owned menu check/radio glyphs have matching source
invariant guards. Shared ModernDialog/ResizingDialog/TitleDlg child controls now
also disable Explorer themed painting in Dark/non-High-Contrast mode instead of
mixing native light chrome with palette-owned surfaces. The elevated NSIS
installer no longer starts fxfile or fxfile-launcher automatically; installed
programs are launched later from the normal user shell/shortcuts so the
application's `asInvoker` contract is preserved. A later screenshot-driven
follow-up also made settings child traversal z-order-safe, stopped enhanced
list/tree controls from restoring Explorer painting over palette-owned Dark
surfaces, and moved the top menu text to explicit ThemePalette drawing so Win11
cannot fall back to black `COLOR_BTNTEXT` on the dark command surface.
A subsequent screenshot pass also gives the Filtering settings list/header an
explicit Dark palette, removes the legacy owner-draw address-field style while
routing `WM_CTLCOLOR` through the real ComboBoxEx parent, and supplies an
explicit Dark disabled image list so Cut/Copy/Undo and other disabled toolbar
commands retain visible glyphs. Root-cause and validation details are recorded
in `docs/development/ERROR_HISTORY.md`.

Latest recorded evidence (2026-09-24): `6b18c58f` corrected an OS-classification
bug that previously routed supported Windows 10/11 through XP-only painters.
The error history records a local `local_ci.bat x64` run using the parent
revision plus that then-uncommitted fix: Release-x64-Unicode compile/link PASS,
native functional regression 17 passed / 0 failed. Its result was PASS WITH
SKIPS because existing HKCU settings caused launch smoke to be SKIPPED / NOT
TESTED. This is not a post-commit revision-matched run; fresh build evidence
for the current checkout and corrected Dark/Light/High Contrast interactive
visual results still require separate confirmation. The `full` profile, x86
compatibility, package validation and installer execution remain inactive
unless the user explicitly expands validation scope. Static, compile/link,
launch, interactive and visual results must remain distinct; DPI at
100/125/150/200%, dialogs, standard/elevated interoperability, resize,
keyboard/IME and final artwork remain separate manual evidence.

Generate the Visual Studio Solution
-----------------------------------
From a Command Prompt in the repository root:

    build.bat

The default is equivalent to:

    build.bat 2026 x64

On first run, build.bat automatically clones the pinned gyp-next version into:

    tools\gyp-next

gyp-next 0.22.2 supports Visual Studio 2026. The build script removes stale
generated Visual Studio projects before regeneration and verifies that the new
.vcxproj files use the v145 platform toolset. Because gyp-next no longer
converts the legacy UseOfMFC attribute, the post-generation fixer writes the
equivalent modern MSBuild UseOfMfc=Static setting into the MFC application
projects.

The root fxfile.vcxproj is an aggregate project used only to group the solution.
It now exposes the same x86/x64 solution configuration names as the real build
projects so Visual Studio can load Release-x64-Unicode and Debug-x64-Unicode
without configuration-mismatch errors.

The main application, xpr, and crash module have native x64 configurations.
The legacy updater, launcher, and keyboard-hook helper are intentionally Win32
projects. When an x64 solution is generated, build\fix_solution_configs.py
maps the launcher and keyboard hook to Win32. The updater is kept loadable but
is not built as part of an x64 solution build because it depends on the x86
multibyte xpr library. Build Release-x86-Multibyte when you need the updater
for packaging. The root aggregate project is also excluded from Build Solution
because all real projects are already listed individually.

If you previously generated fxfile.sln with Visual Studio 2015/v140 or with an
older version of this build script, simply run:

    build.bat

again. Stale generated solution/project files, including the root aggregate
fxfile.vcxproj, are removed automatically before regeneration.

After generation, open:

    fxfile.sln

Build fxfile
------------
For the recommended 64-bit release build, select:

    Configuration: Release-x64-Unicode
    Platform:      x64

Then run "Build Solution" in Visual Studio. The x64 build produces the main
application, x64 xpr/crash modules, and the Win32 launcher/keyhook helpers.
The updater is intentionally deferred; build Release-x86-Multibyte separately
when preparing a full distribution.

The main executable is generated under:

    bin\x64\fxfile.exe

Prepare Runtime Files
---------------------
build.bat now prepares the bundled runtime libraries and language-resource
directories automatically before you open the generated solution. The helper
post-build steps also create their destination directories and run non-
interactively.

You can still refresh these files manually at any time:

    copy_lib.bat
    copy_languages.bat

After a successful Release-x64-Unicode build, run:

    bin\x64\fxfile.exe

Distribution
------------
The `dist` directory contains the release staging, portable-package, and NSIS
installer scripts. A full package requires all three verified release builds:
`Release-x64-Unicode | x64`, `Release-x86-Unicode | Win32`, and
`Release-x86-Multibyte | Win32`; the multibyte build provides the Win32 update
checker that is staged under each platform's `upchecker` directory.

After those builds are available under `bin`, run from the repository root:

    dist\package.bat

Packaging uses the vendored `dist\7zip\7za.exe` and
`dist\nsis\makensis.exe`, so the package build does not download an external
NSIS toolchain. It creates:

    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev.exe
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x64_portable.zip
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x86_unicode_portable.zip
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x86_multibyte_portable.zip

`build\validate_package.ps1` validates the staged runtime trees and portable
ZIP payloads, verifies the expected main executable and x86 upchecker PE
architecture, rejects wrong XPR runtimes, stale updater names, nested staging,
unsafe/archive-traversal paths, duplicate or case-colliding entries and
development artifacts such as PDB/OBJ/LIB/project files, and checks that the
NSIS installer was actually produced. Packaging is no longer
run or uploaded by GitHub Actions; build the required release configurations and
run `dist\package.bat` plus `build\validate_package.ps1 -DistDir dist` locally
when preparing a release.

The installer stages the precompiled legacy `dist/fxfile.chm`.
Editing `docs/htmlhelp/Html/flyExplorer/requirements.htm` does not regenerate
that binary. Its user-facing help remains historical until a separately
authorized Windows help/package rebuild and inspection are performed.

Package Build/Payload PASS is not clean-install evidence. The repository now
contains `build\installer_smoke.ps1` plus an NSIS
`/FXFILE_SMOKE=<temp-dir>` mode. The default smoke only inspects that safety
contract. With `FXFILE_INSTALLER_SMOKE=1` in an elevated local shell,
`local_ci.bat install`/release/full may install only into a temporary directory,
verify the installed payload and main/upchecker architecture, run the protected
runtime smoke, confirm Registry/shell/startup plus Desktop/Start-Menu/Quick
Launch and temporary profile sentinels did not change, preserve pre-existing
fxfile processes and sibling files, silently uninstall, and verify cleanup. That isolated smoke deliberately skips real product Registry,
Start-Menu/Desktop, launcher-startup, and shell-context registration, so a real
clean Windows Sandbox/VM install and shell-integration UX remain separate manual
evidence.

Legacy build
------------
The original project used a customized fxfile/gyp fork, Python 2.7.4, and
Visual Studio 2015/v140. Those instructions are retained only in repository
history; the default build path now targets Visual Studio 2026/v145.

